## Status of Parts a and b

**Part a** asks whether $\liminf_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \omega_k(n+i) \leq k$ for every $k \geq 1$, where $\omega_k$ counts prime factors $> k$. This is an **open conjecture** of Erdős and Selfridge. The solved lower bound (using $\omega$ instead of $\omega_k$) gives $\liminf \geq k + \pi(k) - 1$, which is consistent with but does not resolve part a (since $\omega \geq \omega_k$, the lower bound for $\omega$ does not directly transfer to $\omega_k$).

**Part b** asks whether $\limsup_{n\to\infty} \left(\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \omega(n+i)\right) \frac{\log\log n}{\log n} = 1$. This is also **open**. The classical single-term result (proved above) gives the $k=1$ case, but the extension to fixed $k \geq 2$ is conjectured but unproven.

Both parts remain open research problems and I cannot prove them.

